WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
so let's get ourselves the very latest  on the crisis from on the ground uh  joining me from the eastern ukrainian  city of la viv alistair bunkel from the  southern port city of odessa nick martin  and in the polish capital warsaw of  course a hub for refugees fleeing the  conflict lisa holland good to see all of  you but alistair let's let let's turn to  you first for an overview of what we  have seen overnight i mean certainly the  latest mod briefing suggesting okay it  may be a slow advance but the russians  do continue to encircle kiev  yeah i think that's absolutely right um  they have in their morning updates that  sense of infamous column outside of kiev  of around 40 kilometers long that got  bogged down for many days well now that  the weather has cleared a little bit and  new satellite imagery uh has been  obtained it looks as though that column  has dispersed somewhat um which means  the russians have probably successfully  taken out  some of the the vehicles within it and  redistributed them  around the ukrainian capital nonetheless  uh the ukrainian armed forces have done  a very very good job at not only slowing  that column down but destroying many of  the vehicles within it but it speaks  really to what looks to be the russian  strategy at the moment and that is to  encircle the capital as it has done uh  with other cities like mario paul and  probably strangle it over a number of  days and use um rather than ground  forces that have been pretty ineffective  thus far things like artillery barrages  to to pummel towns and cities into  submission and  air raid sirens in the middle of the  night waking us up here and across  the country as the fear of more russian  airstrikes still persists  and so the the grinding war goes on but  it is a grinding war that is seeing  russia slowly advancing for now despite  uh the very brave ukrainian resistance  alastair thank you uh well let's move on  to odessa where we'll find nick martin  of course nick odessa clearly a  strategic target for the russians but  the people where you are at preparing  for them  yeah that's right i mean odessa is and  always has been high on the list for  vladimir putin it is a key port on the  black sea it used to be the jewel in the  crown of the soviet union and it would  be strategically and militarily a very  key city for vladimir putin and his  military forces to seize because it  would effectively cut off ukraine from  the sea and it would give the russians a  lot more power of what comes in and out  of this country we know that there is  fighting uh two or so hours up the road  uh east mikalaya they've been constantly  shelling that city for a few days now  and we know that there has been shelling  overnight that again another key uh port  city and so that effort to try and cut  off from the black sea is well underway  and they are  preparing unfortunately for a similar  thing here at odessa yesterday we were  down on the beaches watching um  volunteers filling sandbags they filled  350 000 of them in the last two weeks  and they are now fortifying  shop fronts and buildings and cultural  sites the opera house and there are  barricades on all of the streets those  big iron crosses hedgehogs that are  designed to stop the flow of tanks  coming down the street there are  soldiers everywhere and they are uh very  resilient they're saying that the  russians won't even get here i spoke to  an old man on the street yesterday who  said we'll send the russians back but  the fact is that odessa is on vladimir  putin's list and we know that the  fighting and the shelling is getting  closer and  people here are just bracing themselves  you're in a humanitarian aid station  yesterday  a place that just three weeks ago was a  kind of trendy food hall is now  preparing itself to bring casualties  food first aid kits when we were filming  the air raid sirens went off we were  ushered down into the basement and a  load of young people volunteers sat  there for about half an hour listening  to a guy play on the guitar a girl in  the corner was crying and  there's a lot of uncertainty here  and so this city i have to say this  morning  is very quiet but when you take a walk  around it is quite unbelievable to see  the barricades and the fortifications  and the troops and the tanks uh  preparing for what might come to odessa  potentially in the next few days there  were unconfirmed reports of russian  warships just off the coast here in the  black sea there some sightings from  western crimea not far from here had  suggested that they'd seen amphibious  boats off the coast that could suggest  that the russian army would mount an  assault from the beaches here which is  why they are being very cautious and  they're looking out to the black sea  wondering whether those russian forces  will attack from there or possibly from  the east  let's leave odessa for now and head over  the the polish border to warsaw we will  find lisa holland and lisa we were  speaking yesterday about the the huge  number of refugees that are pouring over  the border and the problems that that's  creating i suspect that in the 24 hour  since we last spoke well things haven't  gotten any better  no and in fact whilst there's absolutely  no doubt at all that there is genuinely  a warm welcome here and real real  empathy for what uh people are going  through those who fled across the border  the authorities are not trying to dress  it up as anything else other than the  fact that they are under a huge amount  of pressure in fact the mayor has been  tweeting saying uh things are getting  more and more difficult by the day and  that just about sums it up really this  is not a crisis that's kind of happened  and now they're dealing with the  aftermath this is ongoing this is an  emergency  and more and more people are arriving  almost kind of difficult to keep track  of the numbers but we know that you know  two and a half million people have uh  poured over the borders into  neighbouring countries and the lion's  share have come here to poland and what  that means is that along with volunteers  and the authorities in warsaw they need  to literally kind of pick up the pieces  of people who are getting off trains and  buses absolutely exhausted across on the  border you've got people kind of coming  over on cars  walking over so they they literally kind  of need something to eat something to  drink somewhere to sleep so that's kind  of the initial emergency couple of days  but then it's only just begun because  most of the people that i've been  talking to here don't want to go on to  another country it's not like a kind of  normal migration crisis where people say  right i want to kind of rebuild my life  and end up in the uk because i've got  relatives there or something like that  some people of course do have relatives  but most people just kind of want to  stay put here so they can get back home  as quickly as possible and the key thing  of course is that kind of as we all know  by now the um the men the husbands the  brothers the uh the the fathers they've  stayed behind so women and children here  this is a women and children refugee  crisis uh they all want to sort of get  back home and they're absolutely frantic  and worried sick and just trying to get  a text message or something which says  to them look i'm okay our house is okay  we've spoken to lots of people who've  said look i've had a message to say you  know my house is gone i can't get hold  of my husband my husband's in the army  etc etc it goes on and on and on and so  you know in a nutshell the authorities  here having to kind of pick up the  pieces with all of that to the point now  that there's talk of trying to bus  people to other cities and inevitably  there'll be questions from the polish  government about what the rest of europe  can also do now to support poland  lisa holland in war sonic martin odessa  and of course alster bunker in levitt  thank you all

